## Claim
Text: "Russia sent medical aid to the US, in the form of medical equipment to help coronavirus patients."
Claim author: Dmitry Peskov
Claim date: October 21, 2020

## Initial Q&A
### Did the Russian government send a shipment of medical equipment to the United States in 2020 to assist with the COVID-19 pandemic?
Answer: Yes, the Russian government did send a shipment of medical equipment to the United States in 2020 to assist with the COVID-19 pandemic. According to the search result, the Russian Defense Ministry confirmed that "[a] Russian Air Force plane with medical masks and medical equipment on board departed for the United States" following a phone call between Vladimir Putin and Donald Trump regarding the severity of the pandemic.

Source URL: https://www.aa.com.tr/en/health/russia-sends-medical-aid-to-us-to-help-fight-covid-19/1788290

### Was the medical equipment sent by Russia to the United States in 2020 provided as a free humanitarian donation, or was it purchased by the United States government?
Answer: According to the provided source, there is a dispute over how the medical equipment sent by Russia to the United States in April 2020 was funded. While Russian state media initially described the shipment as "humanitarian aid," the "U.S. later insisted it had purchased the supplies," and the "Kremlin now says the two sides split the bill."

Source URL: https://www.npr.org/sections/coronavirus-live-updates/2020/05/21/860143691/u-s-sends-ventilators-to-russia-in-5-6-million-coronavirus-aid-package

### What specific types of medical equipment and supplies were included in the shipment sent from Russia to the United States in April 2020?
Answer: According to the provided record, the shipment sent from Russia to the United States in April 2020 included "45 ventilators," "4,000 M-95 'full face masks with filters'" (which are military-grade gas masks), "15,000 respirators, some 80,000 packs of skin antiseptic, 30,000 surgical gloves and about 400,000 pieces of medical clothing," as well as household cleaning gloves.

Source URL: https://abcnews.go.com/Politics/russia-bills-us-660k-aid-included-gas-masks/story?id=70451912

### Were the ventilators included in the Russian medical shipment to the United States in April 2020 ever deployed and used to treat COVID-19 patients in American hospitals?
Answer: No, the ventilators included in the Russian medical shipment were never deployed or used to treat patients in American hospitals. According to the search result, the Aventa-M ventilators "were never used" because "they required an electrical voltage not compatible in the U.S." and, due to a flattening curve, "were not needed nor were they ever deployed to hospitals." Ultimately, the Federal Emergency Management Agency (FEMA) "disposed of [them] following strict hazardous waste guidelines" without them ever being utilized.

Source URL: https://www.forbes.com/sites/danielcassady/2020/10/19/the-us-has-thrown-out-faulty-ventilators-russia-sent-as-pandemic-aid/

### Did the United States Federal Emergency Management Agency (FEMA) reject, store, or discard any of the medical equipment, specifically the Aventa-M ventilators, sent by Russia in April 2020?
Answer: Yes, the Federal Emergency Management Agency (FEMA) stored and ultimately discarded the 45 Aventa-M ventilators sent by Russia in April 2020. Upon arrival, the ventilators were "distributed to warehouses in New York and New Jersey," but they were never used or deployed to hospitals because they required an incompatible electrical voltage and presented safety concerns after the same model burst into flames in Russia. Consequently, a FEMA spokesperson confirmed that the ventilators were eventually "disposed of following strict hazardous waste guidelines."

Source URL: https://www.forbes.com/sites/danielcassady/2020/10/19/the-us-has-thrown-out-faulty-ventilators-russia-sent-as-pandemic-aid/

### Were there technical compatibility issues, such as voltage differences, that prevented the Russian-made ventilators sent to the United States in April 2020 from being used in American hospitals?
Answer: Yes, there were technical compatibility issues that prevented the Russian-made ventilators from being used in American hospitals. Specifically, the Aventa-M ventilators "required an electrical voltage not compatible in the U.S., which means they couldn’t be used in the hospitals without an adapter that wasn’t readily available."

Source URL: https://www.forbes.com/sites/danielcassady/2020/10/19/the-us-has-thrown-out-faulty-ventilators-russia-sent-as-pandemic-aid/

### Did the Russian manufacturer of the Aventa-M ventilators sent to the United States in April 2020 face safety recalls or investigations due to hospital fires in Russia?
Answer: Yes. Following deadly hospital fires in Moscow and St. Petersburg that were potentially caused by the equipment short-circuiting, "Russia has now suspended the use of Aventa-M ventilators throughout the country." Additionally, Russian authorities, including the Prosecutor General's Office, launched investigations to establish the causes of the fires and determine if the ventilators played a role in the tragedies.

Source URL: https://www.cnbc.com/2020/05/13/russian-ventilators-sent-to-us-held-after-st-petersburg-hospital-fire.html

### How did the United States Department of State and the Russian Ministry of Foreign Affairs officially characterize the financial terms of the April 2020 medical equipment shipment?
Answer: The United States Department of State characterized the transaction as an outright purchase, with a spokesperson stating that "The United States is purchasing the supplies and equipment outright" and expressing appreciation for Russia "selling these items to us below market value." In contrast, the spokesperson for the Russian Ministry of Foreign Affairs, Maria Zakharova, characterized the financial terms as a split cost, stating that "half of the cargo was paid for by the Russian Direct Investment Fund – a state-owned sovereign wealth investment fund – and half by the US."

Source URL: https://www.cnn.com/2020/04/02/politics/russia-medical-supplies-us-propaganda/index.html

### Did the United States government pay approximately $660,000 or any other amount to Russia for the medical supplies delivered in April 2020?
Answer: According to the provided search result, the exact dollar amount paid by the United States is not specified, but the text confirms that a payment was made, noting that "The U.S. later insisted it had purchased the supplies" and "The Kremlin now says the two sides split the bill" for the medical equipment delivered in April 2020.

Source URL: https://www.npr.org/sections/coronavirus-live-updates/2020/05/21/860143691/u-s-sends-ventilators-to-russia-in-5-6-million-coronavirus-aid-package

## Final Judgement
### Step 1: Summary of Key Insights
The recorded evidence confirms that in April 2020, Russia sent a shipment of medical equipment to the United States, which included ventilators, masks, respirators, and other protective gear. However, the characterization of this shipment as "medical aid" is highly disputed; the U.S. government maintained that it purchased the supplies outright, whereas Russia claimed the two countries split the cost. Furthermore, the 45 Aventa-M ventilators included in the shipment were never used to treat American COVID-19 patients. They were technically incompatible with the U.S. electrical grid and posed severe safety risks, as the same model had caused fatal hospital fires in Russia. Consequently, the Federal Emergency Management Agency (FEMA) stored and eventually discarded the ventilators as hazardous waste without ever deploying them. 

Because the retrieved evidence is clear and comprehensive, no further investigation is required.

### Step 3: Conclusion
Based on the documented evidence, the claim is classified as `cherry-picking`. While it is technically true that Russia sent a shipment of medical equipment to the U.S. during the pandemic, presenting this as "medical aid" to "help coronavirus patients" is highly misleading because it omits crucial context. The transaction was not a purely charitable donation, as the U.S. paid for the supplies (either in full or in part). More importantly, the most critical equipment sent—the ventilators—never helped any patients; they were completely unusable due to voltage incompatibility, posed a fire hazard, and were ultimately thrown away by FEMA. Excluding this context creates a significantly false impression of successful humanitarian cooperation.

### Verdict: CHERRY_PICKING

### Justification
While Russia did send a shipment of medical equipment to the United States in April 2020 ([Anadolu Agency](https://www.aa.com.tr/en/health/russia-sends-medical-aid-to-us-to-help-fight-covid-19/1788290)), characterizing it as "medical aid" to help patients is highly misleading. First, the financial terms of the transaction were heavily disputed; the U.S. government maintained it purchased the supplies outright, whereas Russia claimed the two countries split the cost ([NPR](https://www.npr.org/sections/coronavirus-live-updates/2020/05/21/860143691/u-s-sends-ventilators-to-russia-in-5-6-million-coronavirus-aid-package), [CNN](https://www.cnn.com/2020/04/02/politics/russia-medical-supplies-us-propaganda/index.html)). Second, the shipment included military-grade gas masks and 45 Aventa-M ventilators ([ABC News](https://abcnews.go.com/Politics/russia-bills-us-660k-aid-included-gas-masks/story?id=70451912)), which were never used to treat American COVID-19 patients. These ventilators were technically incompatible with the U.S. electrical grid and posed severe safety risks after the same model caused fatal hospital fires in Russia ([CNBC](https://www.cnbc.com/2020/05/13/russian-ventilators-sent-to-us-held-after-st-petersburg-hospital-fire.html)). Consequently, the Federal Emergency Management Agency (FEMA) stored and ultimately discarded them as hazardous waste without ever deploying them ([Forbes](https://www.forbes.com/sites/danielcassady/2020/10/19/the-us-has-thrown-out-faulty-ventilators-russia-sent-as-pandemic-aid/)). Therefore, the claim is classified as cherry-picking because it omits critical context regarding the payment and the complete failure to utilize the equipment.